{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "96",
    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and hypercholesterolemia under treatment arrived at the emergency department due to sudden onset of substernal chest pain that extended to her neck and jaw. The pain began 2 hours prior, following an incident where she nearly drowned and experienced intense fear. Upon physical examination, her heart rate was recorded at 100 bpm, blood pressure at 150/90 mmHg, and oxygen saturation at 97%. Additionally, a grade 1–2/6 systolic murmur and a fourth heart sound were noted.\n\nECG demonstrated ST-segment elevation in leads V2–V6. Echocardiographic evaluation depicted decreased LV ejection fraction (40%), with new regional wall motion abnormalities, i.e. hypokinesis of the basal and mid segments of the LV. Coronary angiography (CAA) revealed neither major atherosclerotic lesions nor coronary spasm. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) showed no evidence of myocarditis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
